    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my tulsi leaves turning yellow while the soil stays wet?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yellow tulsi leaves with soil that stays wet usually mean waterlogged soil. Holy basil does poorly in waterlogged soil (NC State Extension), and Clemson Extension says roots that sit in water cannot take up oxygen and may rot."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my tulsi tall and leggy with few leaves?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A leggy tulsi is usually short of light. Holy basil needs full sun, and in a dim spot or an AC room the stems stretch towards the light. Tulsi that is never cut back also grows tall and sparse."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are there tiny white flies and sticky leaves on my tulsi?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Tiny white insects that fly up when you touch a tulsi plant are usually whiteflies. Colorado State University Extension describes them as gnat-like and covered in fine white wax; they suck sap and leave sticky honeydew, and heavily infested plants can drop leaves. Aphids also leave sticky honeydew."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my tulsi losing leaves in winter?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A tulsi that loses leaves in winter has usually had cold nights. Basil does not tolerate cold temperatures and is damaged by frost (Iowa State Extension). Coastal UAE winters are mild, but inland and in colder GCC cities, nights can get cold enough to damage it."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my tulsi flowering so much and making fewer leaves?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Tulsi flowers a lot because it is an annual or short-lived perennial that sets seed readily. Flowering is normal, but once seed forms, much of the leaf flavour fades (Iowa State Extension, for basil)."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What is the difference between Rama and Krishna tulsi?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Rama, Krishna and Vana are the three tulsi types named by UT Gardens. Krishna tulsi is the dark, purple type (Cohen, 2014). Rama tulsi has light purple flowers and is the type most common in North American gardens (UT Gardens). Vana tulsi is the white, wild type (Cohen, 2014)."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is tulsi sacred?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "In Hinduism, tulsi is worshipped as a goddess and every part of the plant is considered sacred. Many Hindu households keep a tulsi plant, often in an ornate earthen pot in a courtyard (Cohen, 2014). UT Gardens notes that prayers can begin with an offering of tulsi leaves, and that it is common to ask permission from the plant before harvesting."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can a tulsi plant stay outside in the UAE summer?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes, a tulsi plant can stay outside in the UAE summer, because it likes heat. Give it morning sun and afternoon shade, and test the soil more often in heatwaves, because small pots dry out fast. A bigger pot holds water for longer than a small one."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What is the difference between tulsi and sweet basil?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Tulsi (Ocimum tenuiflorum) and sweet basil (Ocimum basilicum) are two species of basil. Tulsi has hairy green or purple stems, fuzzier and often purple-tinged leaves, smaller flowers and a spicy flavour of clove, mint and basil. Both need full sun and moist, well-drained soil."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can tulsi grow in the shade?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Tulsi does not grow well in the shade. Holy basil needs full sun, six or more hours of direct sun a day, and in shade it gets leggy with few leaves. In the UAE summer, afternoon shade is helpful, but the plant still needs direct sun for the rest of the day."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How long does a tulsi plant live?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A tulsi plant is an annual or short-lived perennial (NC State Extension), so it does not live for many years like a shrub. Tulsi seeds itself easily, so you can keep new plants coming from its seeds."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can you eat tulsi leaves?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes, tulsi is a culinary herb. NC State Extension says it is used in Asian cooking in the same ways as sweet basil, usually cooked or steeped as a tea rather than eaten raw. Iowa State Extension notes its use in some Thai cooking."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does tulsi spread on its own?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes, tulsi seeds itself easily, and new seedlings can come up around the plant. NC State Extension notes that holy basil can be an aggressive weed outside its native range and advises growing it in a container or a confined area."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How is plant size measured?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Size is measured from the bottom of the nursery pot to the top tip of the highest leaf. A plant listed as 50-60 CM stands in that range measured this way."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Which pot option should I choose, nursery or decorative?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Choose the nursery pot for the lowest price or if you already have a planter. Choose a decorative pot for a ready-to-display plant that arrives fully potted with soil mix, perlite and a drainage layer. The plant itself is the same."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is the plant healthy on arrival? Can I check first?",
